# `for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) { const x = a[i]; if (x > 1) s += x; }`, where `n` is the length of a value of which nothing is known: the counter is asked for an int32 in front of the loop and the list under it is asked there too.
# The typed copy had ONE other copy: the closed one, walked from an entry whose parameters are as wide as the look says: there the counter is no integer. Every way out of the typed copy led there, in front and in a turn:
# an element that is no number, met at the second of 64 turns, cost 21% more than the loop had cost when the counter alone was asked.
# Now the other copy of such a purchase is walked as a loop like any other from that same entry, without the list's question, and is bought by itself: it asks the counter in front and has the closed copy behind it.
